{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, deprecated whole when this lands (plan section 1).\n     8\tSlices and companion edits are in\n     9\t[`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    12\t\n    13\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    14\t\n    15\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    16\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    17\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    18\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    19\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    20\t\n    21\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    22\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    23\t> client.\n    24\t\n    25\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    26\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    27\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    28\tcontract beyond the five amendments listed in implementation plan section 2\n    29\t(OD-1, OD-2, OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    30\tsentence).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    33\t\n    34\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    35\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n    36\t  reachable address is required (section 4).\n    37\t- The unreachable cases are named by mapping and filtering behavior, not\n    38\t  product name: endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a\n    39\t  symmetric NAT; the strict paths of a carrier-grade NAT, which is address\n    40\t  sharing and not one behavior, since other carrier-grade paths hole-punch)\n    41\t  defeats punching, and relay is the workaround (section 4).\n    42\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    43\t- Every peer needs an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay.\n    44\t  If UDP is blocked and no such path exists, communication is impossible.\n    45\t- Everything a channel carries is readable by every peer that carries or\n    46\t  receives it until encryption is composed, and the encryption this version\n    47\t  specifies is value-level only: a relay still sees every envelope,\n    48\t  identities, ops, cursors, ids, sizes and timing included (section 7).\n    49\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    50\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    51\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    52\t\n    53\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    54\tnames the seam; `dao.shibi.md` owns the rest); channel-level encryption\n    55\t(deferred, section 7); WebRTC data channels; browser inbound; readiness\n    56\tnotification; exactly-once delivery.\n    57\t\n    58\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n    59\t\n    60\tOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\n    61\tas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\n    62\toperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\n    63\toutcome maps returned verbatim. Four concepts.\n    64\t\n    65\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    66\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    67\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    68\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    69\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    70\t  peer (3.3).\n    71\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    72\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    73\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    74\t  state).\n    75\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    76\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    77\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    78\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    79\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    80\t  share.\n    81\t\n    82\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    83\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    84\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    85\tend.\n    86\t\n    87\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    88\t\n    89\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    90\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    91\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    92\t\n    93\t```clojure\n    94\t;; request\n    95\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n    96\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor | :dao.stream/cursor\n    97\t                                       ; | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    98\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel reader the asker holds\n   100\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n   101\t\n   102\t;; answer, the ordinary case: the contract's outcome map for the requested\n   103\t;; operation, verbatim, plus\n   104\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   105\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   106\t :dao.stream.remote/surface  S         ; descriptor answers only: the entry's declared surface\n   107\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further outcome maps in order\n   108\t\n   109\t;; answer, a protocol error: one shape for every operation, a key instead of\n   110\t;; an outcome map, because no operation's outcome set can carry it\n   111\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   112\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   113\t :dao.stream.remote/error    e}        ; one of the three errors below\n   114\t```\n   115\t\n   116\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   117\tother end's. The request map is open: keys not named here pass to the mirror\n   118\tstep's middleware chain (section 7) and are otherwise ignored. A value that\n   119\tis neither a well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as a\n   120\tdiagnostic below the mirror step and the link, as malformed wire input\n   121\t(`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries). Wire ops are exactly four: `close!`\n   122\tnever crosses, because the mirror holds nothing per remote party to close\n   123\tand the owner's close is never remote; `attach!` never crosses, because it\n   124\tconsumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   125\t\n   126\t**Protocol errors.** The mirror answers three errors with the error key,\n   127\tnever by fabricating an outcome the operation's closed set forbids\n   128\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention; Surfaces):\n   129\t\n   130\t- `not-found`: the identity is absent from the table, whether never served,\n   131\t  retired by policy, or reclaimed with its lease. An identity this peer does\n   132\t  not serve has no operations and no surface, so this one error is the\n   133\t  honest answer for all four ops.\n   134\t- `no-surface`: the op is outside the entry's declared surface, under the\n   135\t  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n   136\t  `:writer`, `descriptor` needs no surface and is always answerable.\n   137\t- `oversize`: the channel writer refused the answer value itself\n   138\t  (`invalid-value`); the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past\n   139\t  it. Large media travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`,\n   140\t  Granularity).\n   141\t\n   142\tAll three names live under `:dao.stream.remote/`. The declared surface is\n   143\tanswer data, not descriptor data: the remote descriptor (2.2) carries no\n   144\tsurface, and the `descriptor` answer carries it under\n   145\t`:dao.stream.remote/surface`, the only place the surface is learned.\n   146\t\n   147\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   148\t\n   149\t```clojure\n   150\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n   151\t :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n   152\t :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n   153\t```\n   154\t\n   155\tThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\n   156\tsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\n   157\tthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\n   158\tcontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\n   159\tserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\n   160\ttransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n   161\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   162\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   163\t\n   164\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   165\t\n   166\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   167\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   168\t\n   169\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n   170\t   with `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/not-found`.\n   171\t2. Present, but the op outside the entry's declared surface under 2.1's\n   172\t   mapping: append the answer with\n   173\t   `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`.\n   174\t3. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   175\t   directly, adding `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; and apply\n   176\t   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n   177\t   through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   178\t   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   179\t   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`. The budget\n   180\t   chase below runs the same `next` through the same path.\n   181\t4. Append the answer with `:dao.stream.remote/id` and\n   182\t   `:dao.stream/identity`.\n   183\t\n   184\tFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\n   185\tcursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\n   186\tfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\n   187\toutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\n   188\tnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\n   189\treader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\n   190\tbudget is correct. A value the channel cannot carry is answered with\n   191\t`:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/oversize` in place of that\n   192\telement (2.1).\n   193\t\n   194\tSteps 1 to 4 are the mirror's only contacts with the entry's handle: no\n   195\tanswer path calls a handle outside `apply-request`, so nothing bypasses the\n   196\tentry's middleware or its declared surface, and a reflection mints every\n   197\tanchor, `:oldest` and `:newest` included, by request.\n   198\t\n   199\tThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n   200\toutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n   201\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   202\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   203\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   204\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   205\t\n   206\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   207\t\n   208\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   209\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   210\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   211\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   212\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   213\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   214\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   215\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   216\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   217\t\n   218\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   219\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   220\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   221\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   222\t\n   223\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   224\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   225\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   226\tfiling each protocol error under its id. Answers whose id is not\n   227\toutstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics.\n   228\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   229\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   230\t\n   231\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   232\t\n   233\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   234\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   235\t  different reachability.\n   236\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   237\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   238\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   239\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   240\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   241\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   242\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   243\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   244\t  yet.\n   245\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   246\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   247\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   248\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   249\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   250\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   251\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   252\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   253\t\n   254\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   255\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   256\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   257\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   258\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   259\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   260\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   262\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   263\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   264\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   265\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   268\tprojection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n   269\tend (3.3), or the medium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding ids, reports each\n   270\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   271\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   272\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   273\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   274\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   275\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   276\t\n   277\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   278\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   279\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   280\t\n   281\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   282\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   283\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   284\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   285\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   286\thandle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n   287\toutcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n   288\tanswers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n   289\trelayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n   290\tthe event writer.\n   291\t\n   292\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   293\t\n   294\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   295\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   296\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   297\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   298\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   299\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   300\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   301\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   302\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   303\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   304\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n   305\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   306\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   307\t\n   308\t## 3. Channels\n   309\t\n   310\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   311\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   312\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   313\tper channel.\n   314\t\n   315\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   316\t\n   317\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   318\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   319\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   320\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   321\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   322\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   323\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   324\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   325\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   326\t\n   327\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   328\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   329\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   330\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   331\t`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n   332\tkeeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n   333\t`:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n   334\t`:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n   335\t(`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n   336\t`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n   337\tloss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n   338\tcomposition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n   339\t\n   340\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   341\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   342\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   343\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   344\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   345\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   346\t\n   347\t### 3.2 UDP\n   348\t\n   349\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   350\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   351\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   352\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   353\t\n   354\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   355\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   356\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   357\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   358\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   359\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   360\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   361\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   362\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   363\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   364\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   365\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   366\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   367\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   368\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   369\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   370\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   371\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   372\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   373\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   374\t  in a payload.\n   375\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   376\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   377\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   378\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   379\t  republishes.\n   380\t\n   381\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   382\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   383\t\n   384\t### 3.3 Pair\n   385\t\n   386\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   387\t\n   388\t```clojure\n   389\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   390\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   391\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   392\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   393\t```\n   394\t\n   395\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   396\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   397\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   398\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   399\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   400\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   401\tunder the inboxes' retention. **An `in` gap ends the channel.** The pair\n   402\tadapter holds the reading cursor on `in`; when `next` there answers `gap`,\n   403\tframes were lost and the adapter cannot say which, so it closes the channel\n   404\treader for this link. The link observes `end` and runs its channel-loss\n   405\tpath (2.4): outstanding ids are abandoned, not re-sent, each abandoned\n   406\t`append!` is reported `append-unknown`, and reattachment is the caller's,\n   407\tby `attach!` on the same pair descriptor, which mints a fresh reading\n   408\tcursor on `in` at `:newest`. The gap is never reported to an inner reader\n   409\tas a source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside\n   410\tan outer `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   411\t\n   412\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   413\t\n   414\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   415\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   416\tthe workarounds conventions. One peer, M below, is reachable and serves two\n   417\tordinary streams from its table; which peer that is carries no protocol\n   418\tmeaning, and any reachable peer can take its place.\n   419\t\n   420\t- One side receives unsolicited (public address, forwarded port, or\n   421\t  endpoint-independent mapping and filtering): direct; the other side dials,\n   422\t  WebSocket or UDP.\n   423\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping or filtering on one or both sides, punching\n   424\t  still possible: hole punch through M (below), UDP allowed.\n   425\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a symmetric NAT;\n   426\t  the strict carrier-grade paths): punching is unreliable; relay through a\n   427\t  pair served by M.\n   428\t- UDP blocked: WebSocket only, to a reachable peer, and the browser rule\n   429\t  applies; without one, impossible (section 1).\n   430\t- Browser at one end: it dials, and two browsers relay through a pair. A\n   431\t  WebRTC data channel is a future channel type whose candidates ride the\n   432\t  same meeting stream.\n   433\t\n   434\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** M serves a `meet-requests` stream with\n   435\tsurface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream with surface `#{:reader}`,\n   436\tboth ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel a peer appends\n   437\t`{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and the channel\n   438\tattachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the reflexive address,\n   439\tand appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h :port p}\n   440\t:dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the board,\n   441\tthen each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   442\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   443\tanswer, even a `not-found` error, carrying one of this peer's outstanding\n   444\tids arrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone\n   445\tconfirms nothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe\n   446\tframe exists.\n   447\t\n   448\t**Bounded meeting work.** M's interpreter bounds its own work: the board's\n   449\tretention (composition data, evict-oldest by default), the number of active\n   450\tpairs in its table, and the fanout of one driver step. A meeting request M\n   451\tcannot honor within its bounds is refused by a gate on its `meet-requests`\n   452\tentry and observed by the asker as `:dao.stream/refused` through its\n   453\treflection: exhaustion is a present answer, not silence. A peer reachable\n   454\tthrough M is reachable only while its channel to M lives, so its keep-alive\n   455\tcadence is bounded by the NAT binding's lifetime, tens of seconds for UDP,\n   456\tlonger for TCP.\n   457\t\n   458\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   459\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table under a lease,\n   460\tand posts their remote descriptors on the board; the asking peers attach a\n   461\tpair channel over them (3.3). M interprets nothing it relays, and that two\n   462\tunreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the network, not a role in\n   463\tthis protocol. Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every reader\n   464\tof the board; this convention claims no private delivery.\n   465\t\n   466\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   467\t\n   468\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   469\t\n   470\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   471\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   472\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   473\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives, per accepted connection,\n   474\t`ws-project` and then `mirror-step` over the projected reader and the\n   475\tsocket handle. B publishes `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n   476\t:dao.stream/identity \"str-1\" :dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`.\n   477\tPeer A calls `attach!`; the link dials and sends the `descriptor` probe;\n   478\tthe answer carries `:dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}`. A calls\n   479\t`cursor` with `:oldest`: no answer filed, the link sends the request and\n   480\tanswers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`; on the next call\n   481\tthe drain files the source's `ok` with `c0`. `next c0` proceeds the same\n   482\tway: `blocked`, then `ok \"hello\" c1`, and `next c1` is `end`. Nothing knew\n   483\tit was a string.\n   484\t\n   485\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   486\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   487\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   488\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   489\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   490\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   491\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   492\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   493\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   494\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   495\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1). The meeting board and\n   496\tthe relay pair are section 4's instances of it.\n   497\t\n   498\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   499\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   500\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   501\t\n   502\t## 6. Lease integration\n   503\t\n   504\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   505\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   506\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   507\t\n   508\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   509\t  it when it likes.\n   510\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   511\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   512\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   513\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   514\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   515\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   516\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   517\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   518\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   519\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   520\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   521\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   522\t\n   523\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   524\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   525\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   526\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   527\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   528\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   529\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   530\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   531\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   532\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   533\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   534\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   535\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   536\t\n   537\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   538\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   539\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   540\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   541\t\n   542\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   543\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   544\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   545\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   546\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   547\t\n   548\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers the `not-found` protocol error for\n   549\ta reclaimed identity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder\n   550\tmay also read the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   551\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   552\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   553\t\n   554\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\n   555\tthe channel's `end` (2.4); the table entry persists, so a holder that\n   556\treconnects within duration plus tolerance finds its entry, its cursors and\n   557\tits lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a\n   558\thealthy channel. Only expiry frees anything. A meeting peer that restarts\n   559\tloses its table and ledger, so every pair is gone, holders observe\n   560\t`not-found` and re-propose; this is `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant\n   561\twith an empty inventory, correct because the table is process-scoped.\n   562\t\n   563\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   564\t\n   565\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   566\t\n   567\tMiddleware preserves positions and outcome kinds as specified in\n   568\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`. Network attachment points are the table entry\n   569\t(gate, value encryption, redaction or metering), the reflection (credential\n   570\tpresentation, value decryption or metering), and the channel (reserved for\n   571\tdeferred channel-level wrapping).\n   572\t\n   573\tKeys and chains are composition data, never descriptor fields. Value-level\n   574\tencryption hides values only; identity, ops, cursors, sizes and timing stay\n   575\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   576\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   577\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   578\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   579\t\n   580\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   581\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   582\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   583\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision. The\n   584\tone ShiBi fit this version chooses: its index interpreter publishes the\n   585\ttuple space's current query value as the decision, and its query\n   586\tinterpreter is `verify`, a query over that value with the request as\n   587\tbindings, the shape `dao.space.query/q` has. `dao.shibi.md` states the\n   588\tadapter contract and its limits (one snapshot, bounded cost, no waiting);\n   589\trequest-specific decisions awaited over streams are a non-goal there.\n   590\t\n   591\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   592\t\n   593\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   594\t\n   595\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   596\tplan lists the successor vocabulary (section 1), the network-path fates\n   597\t(section 5) and the five `dao.stream.md` amendments (section 2), which add\n   598\tno network or capability concept.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
