{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Remote Implementation Plan\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: plan, not rules. The rules are\n     4\t[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md); this file carries the\n     5\tmigration design, the completion criteria and slices, and the companion-edit\n     6\tinventory moved out of that spec to keep it short. Subordinate to the spec\n     7\ton any disagreement.\n     8\t\n     9\t## 1. What replaces `dao.jing.remote`: `dao.jing.content`\n    10\t\n    11\tContent lookup is a request and response service over remote streams\n    12\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 5), in one module family, `dao.jing.content`:\n    13\tthe namespaces `dao.jing.content` (`serve-step`), `dao.jing.content.step`\n    14\t(`step`), `dao.jing.content.driver` (`driver`, clj only) and\n    15\t`dao.jing.content.async` (`async`).\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n    18\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n    19\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n    20\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n    21\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text in\n    22\t  the application value; message boundaries are the channel codec's, one\n    23\t  application value per message, so the vocabulary rides Transit-JSON text\n    24\t  or canonical CBOR alike (`dao.jing.cbor.md`, Remote and DHT).\n    25\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n    26\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n    27\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n    28\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n    29\t  moved from today's private `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by\n    30\t  the interpreter, by the stepped client on every `get` answer and\n    31\t  `:present` verify, and by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one\n    32\t  (today: `dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `dao/jing/remote/step.cljc`, and the\n    33\t  linker's own; only the first includes the canonical CBOR decode).\n    34\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n    35\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n    36\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n    37\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n    38\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n    39\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n    40\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n    41\t  `dao.jing` handle. It replaces `connect-content!`; its consumers are the\n    42\t  coordinate opener and, through it, `dao.space` (the published-index\n    43\t  coordinate opens it at `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`) and `yin.repl.link`\n    44\t  on the JVM. `yin.vm.content` has no remote path today (its handle is an\n    45\t  argument), so it gains one only by being handed a coordinate-built handle.\n    46\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n    47\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API;\n    48\t  `dao.data.btree.storage` is the consumer (`hydrate-async`,\n    49\t  `store-tree-async`).\n    50\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n    51\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n    52\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n    53\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone; its consumers are\n    54\t  the tests and the coordinate itself.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 2. Contract boundary\n    57\t\n    58\tThe `dao.stream.md` changes are the only changes to that contract: OD-1,\n    59\tOD-2 and OD-3; the `:dao.stream/refused` rows on `cursor`, `next` and\n    60\t`append!`; and the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces. They add no network\n    61\tor capability concept. Section 5 holds the fate of every network path;\n    62\tsection 4 lists companion-document edits.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 3. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n    65\t\n    66\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n    67\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n    68\t\n    69\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    70\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n    71\t+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n    72\t| 0  | Contract and source alignment: the `dao.stream.md`         | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and the   | clj cljs cljd |\n    73\t|    | amendments of the spec's section 9 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\n    74\t|    | `:dao.stream/refused` added to the closed outcome sets     | throw; the outcome sets admit the refusal.                   |               |\n    75\t|    | for `cursor`, `next` and `append!` in                      |                                                              |               |\n    76\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`; `dao.stream.observe`,          |                                                              |               |\n    77\t|    | `yin.vm.engine` follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.      |                                                              |               |\n    78\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    79\t| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; a     | clj cljs cljd |\n    80\t|    | `present`, a metering exemplar, an index-interpreter        | gate with the channel allow-list refuses with                |               |\n    81\t|    | exemplar publishing decisions. `src/cljc/dao/stream/        | `:dao.stream/refused`; a gate reads the latest decision      |               |\n    82\t|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | through a capacity-1 medium after eviction; a filter cannot  |               |\n    83\t|    |                                                            | be expressed.                                                |               |\n    84\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    85\t| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the channel;     | clj cljs cljd |\n    86\t|    | remote descriptor dispatch, the protocol errors.           | `gap` from an evicting source crosses verbatim with the      |               |\n    87\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, tests.                  | source's cursor; `blocked` then `ok` on re-ask; `not-found`  |               |\n    88\t|    |                                                            | marks gone; `no-surface` does not; the descriptor answer     |               |\n    89\t|    |                                                            | carries the surface; a kept cursor from a reflection is      |               |\n    90\t|    |                                                            | accepted by the source handle.                               |               |\n    91\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    92\t| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: `ws-project` at each end;   | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj, cljd    | clj cljs cljd |\n    93\t|    | mirror steps over accepted and dialed attachments;         | to clj; both directions serve on one channel; browser        |               |\n    94\t|    | retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and the served-path table    | dials and reads; a closed connection is observed as the      |               |\n    95\t|    | in `dao.stream.ws`.                                        | link's `end`.                                                |               |\n    96\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    97\t| 4  | Content service: the `dao.jing.content` family (section 1) | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the new      | clj cljs cljd |\n    98\t|    | with `serve-step`, `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`;         | module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the linker M3    |               |\n    99\t|    | `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; `dao.jing.coordinate` new         | and M4 tests unchanged in outcome; no require of             |               |\n   100\t|    | coordinate; delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`,        | `dao.jing.remote` remains.                                   |               |\n   101\t|    | `remote/step.cljc`, `remote/async.cljc` and their tests.   |                                                              |               |\n   102\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   103\t| 5  | REPL as a service: `yin.repl.serve` and `connect` over     | `(connect \"daostream:ws://...\")` resolves to a remote        | clj cljs cljd |\n   104\t|    | `requests` and `answers` reflections; delete               | descriptor pair; the demo REPLs work; `yin.repl.link` serves |               |\n   105\t|    | `dao.stream.serving`, `dao.stream.rpc.ws`, the apply wire  | content through slice 4; `dao.stream.rpc` is reworked over   |               |\n   106\t|    | envelope from `dao.stream.apply`; rework or retire         | reflections or retired in the same slice, because the apply  |               |\n   107\t|    | `dao.stream.rpc`.                                          | envelope it requires is deleted here.                        |               |\n   108\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   109\t| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation keyed by  | The toy over UDP with a 100 KiB value; loss injection       | clj cljs cljd |\n   110\t|    | attachment, address, direction and id; adapters            | recovers by resend; oversize beyond the maximum answers      |               |\n   111\t|    | `src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/           | `transport-error` with reason `oversize`.                    |               |\n   112\t|    | stream/udp/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd`.|                                                              |               |\n   113\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   114\t| 7  | Pair channel and the meeting and relay conventions with    | Two peers behind a simulated restricted NAT punch; behind    | clj           |\n   115\t|    | leases: `dao.stream.remote.pair`, `dao.stream.remote.meet`;| a simulated symmetric NAT they relay; a pair whose holder    |               |\n   116\t|    | a meeting gate refuses past the pair bound.                | stops renewing answers `not-found` after duration plus       |               |\n   117\t|    |                                                            | tolerance; a reconnect within it finds the pair intact; a    |               |\n   118\t|    |                                                            | meeting peer past its bound refuses, not silences.          |               |\n   119\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   120\t| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates with a   | clj cljs cljd |\n   121\t|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields `gap` with   |               |\n   122\t|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | the source's cursor.                                         |               |\n   123\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   124\t\n   125\tSlice 7 also proves the pair rule of the spec's 3.3: a `gap` on a pair's\n   126\t`in` ends that link's channel, outstanding appends are reported\n   127\t`append-unknown`, and `attach!` on the same pair descriptor resumes.\n   128\t\n   129\tDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n   130\t(ShiBi), key distribution, channel-level encryption, a WebRTC data channel,\n   131\tbrowser inbound. None changes a shape in the spec's section 2.\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. Companion-edit inventory\n   134\t\n   135\tThe edits the unification requires in other design documents, beyond the\n   136\tspec's own sections. Those not yet made are slices; those made in the\n   137\tauthoring and fix rounds are listed as done.\n   138\t\n   139\t- `dao.stream.md` (done): OD-1, OD-2, OD-3 accepted and moved into the\n   140\t  contract body; the `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and\n   141\t  `append!`; the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces; the OD-3 wording\n   142\t  corrected so `attach!`'s `not-found` and a stale cursor are separate\n   143\t  cases.\n   144\t- `dao.lease.md` (done): served entries and relay pairs named as lease\n   145\t  subjects; the carriage note names the `not-found` protocol error.\n   146\t- `dao.stream.ws.md` (done): the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   147\t  the copy path versus attachment to the original; dialer-side and\n   148\t  acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption and RPC items point to the\n   149\t  spec.\n   150\t- `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (done): 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite\n   151\t  the spec; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's source outcome;\n   152\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the acceptance\n   153\t  row cites slice 8 here.\n   154\t- `dao.jing.md` (done): the coordinate shape and its today/target split;\n   155\t  the deprecated backend bullet; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` marked as the\n   156\t  target; the Base64 carriage named as channel-codec message boundaries.\n   157\t- `yin.vm.linker.md` (done): the content pair vocabulary cites section 1\n   158\t  here; the 6.1 table row, 6.3 shape, M3, deps and failure-policy timing.\n   159\t- `dao.data.btree.md` (done): the 5.4 backend row annotated as the target\n   160\t  name for `dao.jing.remote`; the Phase 4 landing note.\n   161\t- `dao.jing.dht.md` (done): the NAT limitation.\n   162\t- `daostream-udp-design.md` (done): superseded status line.\n   163\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (done): the framing claims corrected to channel-codec\n   164\t  message boundaries with Base64 in the application value.\n   165\t- `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, `dao.space.query.md`,\n   166\t  `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md`\n   167\t  (done): one status sentence each naming the successor.\n   168\t- `dao.jing.hash-registry.md`, `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`\n   169\t  (done): one-line pointers that the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe\n   170\t  today's code and the successor keeps the checks and classifications.\n   171\t- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` (done): the minimum viable stack's content\n   172\t  step marked historical, pointing at `dao.jing.content`.\n   173\t- Remaining, with the code: the `dao.stream.ws.md` residual dialer/acceptor\n   174\t  vocabulary beside its older deposit-model wording, and `dao.stream.md`'s\n   175\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   176\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n   177\t\n   178\t## 5. Unification: the fate of every existing path\n   179\t\n   180\tMoved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n   181\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   182\t\n   183\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   184\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the spec's 3.1\n   185\t  projection. Its accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become\n   186\t  unnecessary once `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is\n   187\t  slice 3.\n   188\t- `dao.stream.serving`: **retired**. Forwards a source into every accepted\n   189\t  socket and flattens a source `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its\n   190\t  consumers are `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote` (which requires and\n   191\t  drives it); both are retired by slices 4 and 5.\n   192\t- `dao.stream.forward`: **unrelated**. A local copy step; replication by\n   193\t  convention, not exposure.\n   194\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: **convention-over**. Correlation by id over two streams\n   195\t  is the spec's section 5 service; its state machine may be kept as one\n   196\t  such vocabulary over reflections, or retired in slice 5.\n   197\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: **retired**. Its only job is translating `:ws/`\n   198\t  envelopes for rpc; the 3.1 projection is the channel binding now.\n   199\t- `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope: **retired**. Its ok-or-error\n   200\t  wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node\n   201\t  and the VM's FFI bridge keep the name and are unrelated to the network.\n   202\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`, the `daostream:ws://`\n   203\t  URL: **convention-over**. An eval service: `requests` and `answers`\n   204\t  streams, `:op/eval` as payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for\n   205\t  that pair. The shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.\n   206\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`, the `:dao.jing/remote`\n   207\t  coordinate in `dao.jing.coordinate`: **retired**. Deprecated whole;\n   208\t  replaced by `dao.jing.content` (section 1).\n   209\t- `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4: **convention-over**. The\n   210\t  content pair is a request and response service over reflections or local\n   211\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.\n   212\t- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`:\n   213\t  **convention-over**. Consume the content client of section 1; the\n   214\t  callback facade is host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   215\t- `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT: **unrelated**. Raw JVM datagram RPC with its\n   216\t  own Kademlia envelope; its socket rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP\n   217\t  channel's template.\n   218\t- `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`: **subsumed**. The channel\n   219\t  codecs, unchanged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
