{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '185,308p'\"","aggregated_output":"   185\tcursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\n   186\tfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\n   187\toutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\n   188\tnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\n   189\treader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\n   190\tbudget is correct. A value the channel cannot carry is answered with\n   191\t`:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/oversize` in place of that\n   192\telement (2.1).\n   193\t\n   194\tSteps 1 to 4 are the mirror's only contacts with the entry's handle: no\n   195\tanswer path calls a handle outside `apply-request`, so nothing bypasses the\n   196\tentry's middleware or its declared surface, and a reflection mints every\n   197\tanchor, `:oldest` and `:newest` included, by request.\n   198\t\n   199\tThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n   200\toutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n   201\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   202\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   203\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   204\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   205\t\n   206\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   207\t\n   208\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   209\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   210\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   211\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   212\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   213\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   214\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   215\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   216\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   217\t\n   218\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   219\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   220\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   221\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   222\t\n   223\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   224\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   225\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   226\tfiling each protocol error under its id. Answers whose id is not\n   227\toutstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics.\n   228\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   229\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   230\t\n   231\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   232\t\n   233\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   234\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   235\t  different reachability.\n   236\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   237\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   238\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   239\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   240\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   241\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   242\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   243\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   244\t  yet.\n   245\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   246\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   247\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   248\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   249\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   250\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   251\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   252\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   253\t\n   254\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   255\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   256\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   257\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   258\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   259\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   260\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   262\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   263\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   264\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   265\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   268\tprojection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n   269\tend (3.3), or the medium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding ids, reports each\n   270\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   271\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   272\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   273\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   274\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   275\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   276\t\n   277\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   278\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   279\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   280\t\n   281\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   282\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   283\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   284\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   285\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   286\thandle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n   287\toutcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n   288\tanswers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n   289\trelayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n   290\tthe event writer.\n   291\t\n   292\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   293\t\n   294\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   295\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   296\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   297\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   298\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   299\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   300\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   301\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   302\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   303\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   304\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n   305\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   306\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   307\t\n   308\t## 3. Channels\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Middleware\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which wins on any disagreement. Every\n     5\tsentence below is a rule. This document names no network; the network\n     6\tattachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n     7\tsection 7.\n     8\t\n     9\t## What a middleware is\n    10\t\n    11\tA middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\n    12\toperation map on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. It\n    13\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    14\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    15\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    16\t\n    17\t```clojure\n    18\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    19\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    23\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    24\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    25\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    26\t\n    27\t## The operation map\n    28\t\n    29\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    30\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    31\t\n    32\t```clojure\n    33\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    34\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    35\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    39\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    40\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    41\t\n    42\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    43\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    44\t\n    45\t## wrap\n    46\t\n    47\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    48\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    49\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    50\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    51\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    52\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    55\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    56\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    57\t\n    58\t## The position rule\n    59\t\n    60\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    61\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    62\t\n    63\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    64\t  cursor;\n    65\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    66\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    67\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    68\t\n    69\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    70\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    71\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    72\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    73\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    74\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    75\tmiddleware.\n    76\t\n    77\t## The four prohibitions\n    78\t\n    79\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over any handle,\n    80\tnever retries, never waits, and never touches the inner handle: the only\n    81\thandle operations it may perform are the decision read `gate` defines below\n    82\t(a bounded read on a composed decision medium) and one `append!` to a\n    83\tside stream it was composed with. It reads no host clock. A transform that\n    84\tneeds facts from a stream is not a middleware: folding a stream is an\n    85\tinterpreter's work, and what a middleware reads is a decision an\n    86\tinterpreter already published onto a medium.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Failure inside a transform\n    89\t\n    90\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n    91\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n    92\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n    93\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n    94\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n    95\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    96\t\n    97\t## Two generic middlewares\n    98\t\n    99\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n   100\tspecified here.\n   101\t\n   102\t```clojure\n   103\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify   verify\n   104\t       :dao.stream.middleware/decision h})\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   108\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   109\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   110\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling. A decision is\n   111\twhatever value the index publishes; the gate reads none of it. It may be an\n   112\timmutable query value, a relation value or an opened published index, in\n   113\twhich case `verify` is a query over it with the request as bindings: the\n   114\tshape `dao.space.query/q` has (a query over immutable inputs, opening\n   115\tnothing), and the required adapter contract for a query interpreter that\n   116\tplugs into the gate (`dao.shibi.md`, How it plugs in).\n   117\t\n   118\t`wrap` calls `cursor` with `:dao.stream/oldest` once when it constructs\n   119\tthe gate. The medium must be open and readable; it has capacity one and\n   120\tevicts its older value. If cursor minting is not `ok`, the gate has no read\n   121\tcursor or decision. Each operation retries `cursor` once; if it is still\n   122\tnot `ok`, `next` is skipped and `verify` receives the none marker. A\n   123\tsuccessful mint is its read cursor.\n   124\t\n   125\tOn each operation, the gate calls `next` once at its read cursor. On `ok`,\n   126\tit stores the successor cursor and value. On `blocked`, it keeps the cursor\n   127\tand last value. On `end`, it clears the value and becomes ended. On `gap`,\n   128\tit clears the value, adopts the recovery cursor, and calls `next` once more.\n   129\tIf that call is `ok`, it stores the successor and value; if `blocked`, it\n   130\tkeeps the recovery cursor without a value; if `end`, it becomes ended; if\n   131\t`gap`, it adopts that recovery cursor and remains without a value. It does\n   132\tnot read a third time in this operation. On any other non-`ok` outcome,\n   133\tit clears the value and cursor, then retries cursor minting next operation.\n   134\t\n   135\t`verify` receives the last value observed, or\n   136\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}` when there is none, or\n   137\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/ended true}` once the gate is ended (the medium\n   138\tclosed; no later operation reads). That value is a bounded snapshot, not a\n   139\tpromise that it is the newest concurrent write.\n   140\t`nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits with\n   141\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   142\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   143\trefuses the marker. The cursor and value are state of the\n   144\twrapped handle, supplied to `wrap`, not global state. The composition must\n   145\tserialize calls on one wrapped handle; the gate has no queue, lock or wait.\n   146\tThe decision medium is not the wrapped stream, so the position rule on the\n   147\tinner handle is untouched.\n   148\t\n   149\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   150\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   151\tit was composed with: this is **decision emission**, and an index that\n   152\tobserves that stream is how metered use keeps its state on a stream\n   153\trather than in the gate.\n   154\t\n   155\t**present**, the credential step.\n   156\t\n   157\t```clojure\n   158\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   159\t```\n   160\t\n   161\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   162\t`out` is identity.\n   163\t\n   164\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   165\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   166\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   167\t[`dao.shibi.md`](./dao.shibi.md).\n   168\t\n   169\t## Exemplars\n   170\t\n   171\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   172\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   173\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   174\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   175\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   176\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   177\t\n   178\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   179\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   180\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   181\t\n   182\t## Invariant compliance\n   183\t\n   184\tNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\n   185\tcontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\n   186\tlater. No implicit control flow: the four prohibitions. No hidden global\n   187\tstate: keys, policies and decision media are arguments to `wrap`. No shared\n   188\tmutable state beyond what any handle holds. Interpretation and execution\n   189\tstay separate: `apply-request` judges no value. No assumed graph: a chain is\n   190\tan explicit vector.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
