{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '1,15p;78,90p;112,123p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoSpace for Agent Collaboration\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design discussion (2026-07-10); a coordinator-mediated prototype ran live the same\n     4\tday, then was **superseded (2026-07-12) by the streams-native model**: there is no\n     5\tcoordinator. Stigmergy is writing datoms to the agent's own local stream via\n     6\t`dao.space.transactor`'s `append!`/`transact!` (one atomic transaction record per call) and\n     7\treading dao.space with `q`/`match` — nothing else. The living contract is\n     8\t`test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj`: agents coordinate over a network-accessible\n     9\t`dao.jing.file` content handle served by `dao.jing.remote/serve-content!`, and the finished space persists at\n    10\t`target/stigmergy-space.db` for inspection with `dao.space.query`.\n    11\tDescribes how `dao.space` serves as a coordination medium for autonomous agents — LLM agents\n    12\tspecifically — and enumerates what exists today versus what is still needed. Nothing here\n    13\tproposes changing the tuple-space model; the model is the point.\n    14\t\n    15\t**Related documents:**\n    78\t4. **Perfect Provenance**: Every summary datom carries an `m` (metadata) entity. This provides an exact, auditable trace of which model generated the summary, at what millisecond, using which system prompt.\n    79\t\n    80\t## What exists today\n    81\t\n    82\tThe substrate is real and tested:\n    83\t\n    84\t- **Shared storage** — `dao.jing` with in-memory, file, WebSocket-remote\n    85\t  (`dao.jing.remote`, `:clj` client), and DHT backends. Agents on different\n    86\t  machines can share a store today.\n    87\t- **Associative read** — `dao.space.query/q` (Datalog joins) and `match`\n    88\t  (positional templates) over exact-bounded DaoStream descriptors or closed\n    89\t  retained realizations. `current` and `history` add explicit d5 interpretation\n    90\t  and optional `as-of` bounds.\n   112\t`dao.space` needs a thin tool server exposing, at minimum:\n   113\t\n   114\t- `query` — run a `q` form or `match` template, return results as EDN/JSON\n   115\t- `deposit` — append datoms (or entity maps, which the library already normalizes) to the\n   116\t  calling agent's own log\n   117\t- `vocabulary` — return the attribute vocabulary in use (see gap 3)\n   118\t\n   119\t`dao.stream.rpc` already provides the transport shape this wants — a WebSocket server\n   120\tdispatching a `{op-keyword fn}` handlers map — so the work is an adapter (MCP's JSON-RPC on\n   121\tone side, `q`/`match`/append on the other), not new infrastructure. Most agent runtimes are\n   122\tPython or TypeScript, which is also why this must be a protocol bridge rather than an\n   123\tembedded library: the Peer stays in-process on the JVM; agents reach it over the wire.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
